Yes—with one important refinement: action doesn’t guarantee confidence; it gives fear a chance to acquire evidence.

In NTE form:

Fear_t
\rightarrow
ACT
\rightarrow
Consequence
\rightarrow
Evidence
\rightarrow
ModelUpdate
\rightarrow
Confidence_{t+1}

Fear is largely prospective:

Fear \sim P(\text{bad outcome})\times Cost(\text{bad outcome})

Confidence is partly accumulated evidence:

Confidence \sim
\text{evidence that I can act, adapt, recover, or endure}.

So the story transformation isn’t simply:

Fear\rightarrow Confidence.

It’s:

\boxed{
Fear
\xrightarrow{\text{Action}}
Experience
\xrightarrow{\text{Metabolism}}
Confidence
}

And failure can still produce confidence. If the protagonist acts, fails, discovers the failure is survivable, learns, and acts again:

Fear
\rightarrow
Action
\rightarrow
Failure
\rightarrow
Recovery
\rightarrow
\text{“I can handle failure”}

That’s often stronger than “I always win.”

Narratively, courage is the bridge state:

\boxed{
Fear \rightarrow Courage \rightarrow Action \rightarrow Evidence \rightarrow Confidence
}

Courage doesn’t require confidence first. That’s why it’s useful.

And it connects beautifully to the previous insight about unbreakability: confidence needn’t mean I won’t break.

It can mean:

I’ve seen what happens when I break, and I know how to move afterward.

That’s a kata you can actually train.